<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yeonsangun of Joseon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1476">1476</a>–<a href="/years/1506">1506</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yeonsangun was the tenth king of Joseon, ruling from 1494 to 1506, and is remembered as its worst tyrant. He carried out the literati purges of 1498 and 1504, the second in revenge for the death of his mother, who had been deposed and forced to take poison. He closed the national academy, banned writing in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Korean alphabet</a> after critical posters appeared, and was deposed in 1506 and denied a temple name.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yeonsangun, personal name Yi Yung, was born on 2 December 1476, the eldest surviving son of King Seongjong and the Deposed Queen Yun. Lady Yun had been Seongjong's concubine and was chosen as his second wife for her beauty. She was jealous of the other concubines, poisoned one of them in 1477, and in 1479 struck the king and left scratch marks. Seongjong's mother ordered her into exile, officials later petitioned for her execution, and she died in 1482, ordered to drink poison. The crown prince grew up believing that he was the son of Queen Jeonghyeon, his father's third wife.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In his early years on the throne Yeonsangun was an able administrator who strengthened the national defence and aided the poor. When he learned the truth about his mother he tried to restore her titles, but officials of the Sarim faction opposed him. In 1498 a passage critical of King Sejo's coup of 1455 was put into the royal records, and the king ordered the execution of many Sarim officials and the mutilation of the remains of Kim Chong-jik: the First Literati Purge.</p><p>In 1504 Im Sa-hong showed him a blood-stained garment from his mother's death. Yeonsangun beat to death two of his father's concubines, and had officials who had backed his mother's execution put to death, and even those merely present at court: the Second Literati Purge.</p><p>He closed the royal university Sungkyunkwan and the temple Wongaksa and made them pleasure grounds, gathering young girls from the eight provinces, and evicted 20,000 residents of the capital to clear hunting grounds. He abolished the Office of Censors. On 19 July 1504 three notices in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangul">Hangul</a> mocking his tyranny were found, and he banned the learning and use of the script and ordered those using it beheaded. The author was never caught, and the ban was short-lived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In September 1506 a group of officials, among them Pak Wŏnjong, Sŏng Hŭian and Yu Sunjŏng, deposed him and set his younger half-brother Grand Prince Jinseong on the throne. Demoted to Prince Yeonsan, he was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganghwa-island">Ganghwa Island</a>, where he died after two months. His four young sons were made to take poison. As a much-despised overthrown king he received no temple name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yeonsangun of Joseon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongbokgung">Gyeongbokgung</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganghwa-island">Ganghwa Island</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.58,126.98&amp;year=1495" aria-label="Gyeongbokgung on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Yeonsangun of Joseon"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5472 4339h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5472 4339h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.58,126.98&amp;year=1495">What was here in 1495? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
